this rally in panama city is a  consequence of a collapsed deal between  a protest movement and the government  it aimed to reduce fuel prices in  exchange for ending two weeks of  demonstrations  [Music]  but these people are calling for fresh  action  the national coordinating committee of  indigenous peoples of panama call on the  entire indigenous population to a  national strike on thursday  the agreement was signed in cherokee  province last week it lowered the price  of fuel by about a third in exchange for  unblocking the pan-american highway a  crucial trade link  the protesters tore up the deal saying  paying less at the pump won't solve a  cost of living crisis or end corruption  banners like this are displayed across  roads this one calls for cheaper  medicine and justice for the poor  the international monetary fund says  recent figures show the economy is  improving since the pandemic but there  is still a high rate of inequality  and the protests are having a direct  impact on supermarket shelves  luckily we got tomatoes but no onions  there is a problem the one supermarket  here that sells vegetables and fruit has  practically closed  as you can see most of the stalls are  closed early because they don't have  anything to sell  let's hope the government and leaders  reach a solution soon protesters say  they will take to the streets until the  prices of basic goods are reduced  but with markets struggling to stock up  panama's four and a half million people  risk going hungry  manly al jazeera
 

